{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789228406,"nonce":"Cge7vU2DaDVdzKiFG7S+KkjWhck=","title":"Runtime seam: let a host override ash_std's collector and scheduler at run time","message":"ash keeps its collector and scheduler and depends on nothing. What it gains\nis a seam, ash_std::rt: a table of the entry points its own modules reach\nthe runtime through, filled with ash's implementations by default and\nreplaceable by a host before the first allocation. caribou, when it hosts\nash, installs its heap and scheduler into the table; ash's main never names\ncaribou, and ash's tests keep running against ash's own code.\n\nSame pattern ash already uses for the closure runner and the switch hook:\nan atomic slot per entry, an installer export, ash's function as fallback.\n\nEntries are the Rust calls other std modules make (gc_alloc, gc_locked,\ngc_safepoint, gc_set_blocking, alloc_with_finalizer, register/unregister\nfiber stack and OS thread, gc_add/remove_persistent, mark_site,\ngc_update_fiber_sp, allocation_size, out_of_memory; park, wake, new_waiter,\nsleep_until, block_yield, schedule_step, thread spawn, poll epoch address,\nblocking depth), not the hlp_* C exports, which stay thin names for them.\n\nSupersedes the branch that made ash_std depend on caribou (kept as a stash\nin the caribou-heap worktree for reference).","files":null}]}